### F9 [issues-11-21] Plan §3.1 'Graph shape' (ZK privacy and FHE plan.md:375) and §15 item 23 (:1982)

**Problem.** The replacement for the revision 4 §15 item 2 wording (#18) is still inaccurate. The plan says 'W1's non-deletion milestones have no ancestor in WG or W2–W7; they depend only on W1, W0 and W6 gate milestones'. The two clauses contradict each other, because W6 is inside W2–W7, and the first clause is false. These milestones have W6 ancestors: W1.0 ← W6.REL.H/W6.REL.D; W1.5 ← W6.M; W1.5i/W1.6i ← W6.H, W6.G; W1.Q.FPQ ← W6.R.FPQ, W6.P, W6.MB; W1.Q.RACE/W1.Q.GEN ← W6.REL.R. The true property is 'no ancestor in WG, W2–W5 or W7'. '#18's corrected fix used exactly that wording. 'W6 gate milestones' also mislabels W6.M, which is instrumentation construction, and the W6.R.* reviews.

**Fix.** Replace the first bullet under §3.1 "Graph shape" (plan:375) with: "W1's non-deletion milestones have no ancestor in WG, W2–W5 or W7 (machine-checked). Outside W1 they depend only on W0 and on W6 infrastructure:
- the release model, certified-history boundary and activation key set (W6.REL.D, W6.REL.H, W6.REL.K);
- the review protocol and review scopes (W6.D0, W6.D0.*);
- the harness, the gate, rollback, instrumentation, the pipeline and the evidence builder (W6.H, W6.G, W6.RB, W6.M, W6.P, W6.MB);
- the external reviews (W6.R.FPQ, W6.R.RACE, W6.R.GEN, W6.REL.R).
These are W1.0–W1.4, W1.1b, W1.M, W1.Q.FPQ, the decisions, and the RaceV1 and generic-registry chains through W1.A.RACE and W1.A.GEN."

In §15 item 23 (plan:1982), change "have no ancestor in WG or W2–W7" to "have no ancestor in WG, W2–W5 or W7 (their only outside dependencies are W0 and W6 infrastructure)".

### F20 [issues-33-43] Plan §8.2 Tests in W5.F (plan:1386-1393); graph W5.F test list (graph W5 section, W5.F row). Lens issue #35.

**Problem.** The plan now enforces R_min on the merged stream of CRL, policy and anchor rotations. The tests never check that property across event types. 'A CRL, policy or anchor rotation inside R_min is rejected' is passed by an implementation that enforces R_min separately per stream. The issue's interleaved-stream test is also absent: each stream on its own inside the band, but the merged spacing below P + X, with starvation as the result.

**Fix.** Add to the W5.F test list in plan §8.2 (after plan:1393) and in the graph W5.F row:

(a) Cross-type R_min:
- a certificate-policy rotation committed less than R_min after a CRL rotation for the same (trust anchor, policy) is rejected, and so is a trust-anchor rotation;
- the reverse orders are rejected too: a CRL rotation within R_min of a policy rotation, and a CRL rotation within R_min of an anchor rotation;
- an anchor rotation is checked against the last superseding commit of every (anchor, policy) pair under that anchor, and is rejected if any of those pairs is inside R_min;
- a revocation of any of the three types inside R_min is still admitted.

(b) Merged-stream starvation: set a test-genesis R_min below P + X. Interleave CRL rotations with policy rotations, or with anchor rotations, for one pair so that each stream alone is spaced at least P + X, and the merged spacing is at least R_min but below P + X. This admits zero proofs over at least 3 cycles. The same schedule with merged spacing of at least P + X admits a proof every cycle.

W5.I1 inherits (a) through its existing "the W5.F ... R_min tests" line (plan:1400). Change that line to name the cross-type cases explicitly. (b) stays in W5.F only.

### F42 [issues-66-76] #71: W5.D5 (graph:463), W5.P (graph:456), plan §8.2 'Prover platforms' (plan:1361-1367); W6.D0.X509 (graph:523), W6.R.X509 (graph:489)

**Problem.** The #71 fix adds a new attack surface without the analysis the issue required. Phones hand the full private witness (certificate chain, subject key, nullifier inputs) to a 'holder-controlled server', and this is a first-class platform that §8.2 recommends qualifying first. The plan requires only that the channel be 'mutually authenticated, end-to-end encrypted'. There is no holder-privacy or transport analysis, which the issue required for delegated proving, and the X509 review scope does not cover the hand-off.

**Fix.** Keep the proposed fix but scope it to what is actually at risk.

1. **W5.D5 hand-off threat model.** Add a short threat model to W5.D5 for the wallet-to-host hand-off. State that soundness and redirection are already covered by local verification in W5.P and by the ownership signature's binding of account, challenge and intent (relation.rs:462-483). The subject private key never leaves the phone. The model then covers:
   - holder privacy against network and host compromise: pairing and enrollment, custody of the channel keys, and revocation of a lost or compromised host;
   - what the host may keep (the witness is erased after the job) and network-metadata linkability;
   - replayed or duplicated jobs, and denial of service against B.

2. **Named channel construction.** Name the channel construction in W5.P instead of the bare phrase "mutually authenticated, end-to-end encrypted".

3. **Review scope.** Add the hand-off protocol to the W6.D0.X509 scope and to the W6.R.X509 coverage list.

4. **Adversarial cases.** Add these to W5.I2, or to a W5.P SDK test:
   - an unpaired or substituted host is rejected;
   - a man in the middle on the channel fails;
   - a replayed job is rejected;
   - a tampered returned proof is rejected by local verification before submission.

5. **Owner decision.** If the owner treats a holder-controlled host as within the holder's own trust boundary, record that as an explicit W5.D3/A9 statement instead of leaving it implicit.

### F45 [issues-77-87] Issue #77. Graph W6.O.4a/4b/4c (graph:539-541), W4.CK (graph:437), W4.D7 (graph:409), W6.O.IVM (graph:538), W3.CK (graph:392), W6.O.WG (graph:536), W1.X2 (graph

**Problem.** The issue is only partly resolved. r5 adds W6.O.<cap> release outcomes and the W3/W4/W5 checkpoints, but for several non-W7 capabilities the descoped branch cannot be reached in the very failure cases the plan names. So W6.IC can still never close if one of these fails, which is exactly what #77 objected to.

**Fix.** 1. Make every W6.O.<cap> descoped branch closable in all the failure cases the plan names:
   - a recorded construction failure;
   - a W4.D7 'no candidate' record;
   - WG.E or WG.BUD evidence;
   - an upstream W6.O descoping.

   Cite the checkpoint (W3.CK, W4.CK, W5.CK) only when it exists. To do this, remove the hard edges W4.CK→W6.O.4c, W3.CK→W6.O.IVM and W5.CK→W6.O.X509, and move each to the matching 'Qualified' alternative text. If a checkpoint closes, it still gates W4.6c, W3.8 and W5.5 as it does now.

2. Add a cascade rule. When an upstream W6.O node records a descoping (W6.O.WG for AXT and IVM; W6.O.4a for 4b, 4c and THR; W6.O.4b for 4c), the downstream W6.O node closes as descoped by citing that record. Edit W6.O.4b and W6.O.4c to accept this in place of 'W4.6b measurements' or 'the W4.CK checkpoint'.

3. Give every non-W7 scope node a descoped closure, modelled on W6.D0.CV: 'or, if its W6.O outcome is descoped before review, by citing that record with no reviewer named'. This covers W6.D0.WG, AXT, IVM, 4a, 4b, 4c, THR, SC and X509. Decouple W6.D0.IVM from WG.1 in that branch.

4. Extend D6 to W1.X2. When IVM or WG is descoped, the descoping record lists every ivm_step_air chip for deletion, and W1.X2's W3.F edge is re-pointed to the W6.O.IVM outcome, on the dead-code path (zero importers). W3.D1 and WG.1 are then not required.

5. Align the plan with the graph:
   - plan §9.8;
   - the plan:1497 row, so it says the checkpoint is cited when it exists;
   - the plan:1894 row.

6. Re-run the machine checks, adding one: for each W6.O node, a descoped closure is reachable from any recorded upstream failure without that capability's construction nodes.

### F70 [facts-3] §6.6 W3.4 row ("Each of the 5 fault kinds") and §6.7 opcode table row "Faults and terminal outcomes" (lines ~927 and ~964)

**Problem.** The plan says IVM has 5 fault kinds: malformed opcode, out of gas, cycle limit, failed assertion and missing HALT. The code has many more runtime trap outcomes. Several of them are not assigned to any W3 milestone or corpus: OutOfMemory, NumericFault, ContractAbort, PermissionDenied, PrivacyViolation, RegisterOutOfBounds, InvalidVectorLength, VectorExtensionDisabled, HostOutputBudgetExceeded, AmxBudgetExceeded, SyscallGasQuoteExceeded, GasCostOverflow, UnknownSyscall, NoritoInvalid and AbiTypeNotAllowed. Memory faults, PointerAbiFault and strict-return traps are only partly covered, through the W3.2 and W3.3 corpora. As written, the terminal-outcome obligation and the W3.4 test standard both under-cover the outcomes the relation has to prove.

**Fix.** 1. Replace the fixed list in §6.7 ("Faults and terminal outcomes") and in the graph's W3.4 row with an outcome matrix derived from `VmTrapKind` (crates/ivm_abi/src/error.rs:31-63) through `classify_trap` (crates/ivm/src/ivm/diagnostic.rs:105-149). It has four classes:
   - **(a) Prepare-time kinds.** `InvalidMetadata`, the three `UnsupportedProgram*` kinds, `ProgramVectorLengthTooLarge`, `ArtifactAbiHashMismatch`, `GenericSyscallNotAllowed`. Admission rejects these, and the relation has no row for them.
   - **(b) Interpreter-raised in-trace kinds**, each assigned to one owner:
     - `DecodeError`/`InvalidOpcode`, including fetch traps → W3.4;
     - `OutOfGas` and `ExceededMaxCycles` → W3.4;
     - `AssertionFailed` → W3.4;
     - `MissingHalt` → W3.4;
     - `MemoryFault` → W3.2;
     - `PointerAbiFault`, `NoritoInvalid`, `AbiTypeNotAllowed` and the strict-return traps → W3.3;
     - `InvalidVectorLength`, `RegisterOutOfBounds`, and `PermissionDenied` from `VectorExtensionDisabled` → W3.6;
     - `PrivacyViolation` → W3.7 (secret-tag traps);
     - `OutOfMemory` from TLV handling → W3.3.
   - **(c) Syscall-raised kinds**, all owned by W3.5: `NumericFault` with all 13 `NumericFaultV1` sub-kinds, `GasCostOverflow`, `UnknownSyscall`, `NotImplemented`, `ContractAbort`, `PermissionDenied`, `HostOutputBudgetExceeded`, `AmxBudgetExceeded` and the syscall `OutOfGas`. W3.5's per-syscall matrix lists the trap kinds each syscall can raise. Its differential corpus reaches each one, and "result" explicitly includes the trap kind.
   - **(d) Host-invariant guards.** `SyscallGasQuoteExceeded` and `SyscallMeteringModeMismatch`. Either show they are unreachable under a correct host, or prove them as outcomes. Record which, as a W3.D3/A9 note.
2. Restate the W3.4 corpus as: "each in-trace trap kind owned by W3.4 in at least 10^5 programs". Drop "5 fault kinds". Give W3.2, W3.3, W3.5, W3.6 and W3.7 the matching "each owned trap kind reached at least N times, 0 outcome divergences" clause.
3. Add a trap-kind completeness test to W3.8, next to its opcode/syscall mapping test. It fails if any `VmTrapKind` variant other than `Other` lacks a class and owning milestone. It also fails if any in-trace kind has no corpus evidence.

### F81 [facts-5] Plan §8.2, 'Prover platforms (W5.D5)', line 1359

**Problem.** The plan says 'Every existing receipt is server-class: a 20-worker VM with 12 GiB RSS and 32 GiB address-space ceilings.' This is wrong. The plan itself cites a Metal maximum (§8.3, line 1416) that ran natively on macOS, not in the Lima VM, and failed the Darwin address-space gate. Several other Darwin-PID receipts exist under dist/zk-x509-prover-evidence. This matters for W5.D5, because a holder-controlled desktop is one of the candidate platform classes.

**Fix.** Replace the first sentence at line 1359 with:

"Every existing Linux receipt is from a 20-worker Lima VM (shared boot_id) under the 12 GiB RSS and kernel-enforced 32 GiB address-space ceilings. The one native macOS datapoint is the epoch25 Metal maximum (`epoch25-mac-deep-capacity-maximum-artifact1`), with these results:
- 20 rayon workers and 2,910 Metal transform calls;
- 1,061.052853 s proving time (`maximum-receipt.txt:110`);
- conservative RSS 8.25 GB, within 12 GiB;
- failed the Darwin address-space gate: the hard limit was not enforced, and the observed lower bound was 508,048,965,632 B (`result.json`);
- failed the time gate (`status.md:175-176`)."

Then add one sentence: "W0.D4 records whether that Mac host is a candidate holder-controlled desktop platform. If it is, W5.D5 and W5.CK treat it as measured and failing, not unmeasured."

Keep the rest of the paragraph and the 12 GiB / 32 GiB ceilings unchanged.

### F88 [facts-6] Plan §15 item 22, line 1981

**Problem.** The claim that "each adapter checks ≤1% unattributed time in its own milestone" is not true in the graph. Several FASTPQ consumers have neither the criterion nor a W6.M dependency.

**Fix.** Make item 22 and §9.5 match the graph.

1. Add the W6.M edge to W4.7, which has the criterion but no W6.M ancestor.
2. Add the W6.M edge and the "prover emits the complete W6.M phase tree with unattributed time at most 1% of wall time" criterion to these FASTPQ provers:
   - W4.5;
   - W4.8, under W4.D8 (a);
   - W4.4, under W4.D8 (a);
   - W1.5b, if W1.D1 chooses migration;
   - W1.6, for its at-least-one registered real relation. Alternatively, state that W1.6 is verifier-only and that W7.ZV.c carries the check for the generic-registry relation.
3. For W4.6a-c, make the criterion conditional on W4.D7 choosing a FASTPQ construction. Otherwise W4.D7 must name an equivalent complete phase attribution with ≤1% unattributed time.
4. Extend the W6.M criterion list and the §9.5 list to match.

Alternatively, narrow item 22 and §9.5 to "each listed adapter (W1.5, W2.5, W3.B, W4.7, W5.1, W7.NF.c, W7.AC.c, W7.ZA.c, W7.ZV.c)". State that W1.6, W4.4, W4.5, W4.6a-c and W4.8 are not checked. Still add the missing W6.M edge to W4.7.

### F127 [critic-security] Graph W1.6 (338), W1.A.GEN (341), W6.R.GEN (481); plan §3.3 W1.6/W1.A.GEN rows (426-430)

**Problem.** After W1.A.GEN, any governance-registered FASTPQ AIR descriptor that is not binding-only is admitted in production through VerifyProof, the IVM host syscalls, Torii and preverify. There is no per-relation review, qualification or signed record. W6.R.GEN claims to cover 'every relation registrable through the generic path', but no milestone closes that set. An under-constrained AIR registered later is an unsound relation that production accepts. One example is the W7.ZV ballot relation before W7.ZV.A, since W7.ZV.A gates only the voting ISIs.

**Fix.** 1. Make the allowlist compiled and record-bound. In W1.6, W1.Q.GEN and W1.A.GEN, the FASTPQ generic route admits only relations on an allowlist compiled into the binary.
- Each entry is the relation's W0.1 typed ID.
- The list is part of GEN's compiled capability digest (A3) and of the W1.Q.GEN record scope.
- W6.D0.GEN (graph:513) and W6.R.GEN (graph:479) review exactly this list, in place of "every relation registrable".

2. Enforce the list at every entry point, not only at registration. These all refuse a relation that is not on the list or whose own W6.G record is not active:
- RegisterVerifyingKey and UpdateVerifyingKey;
- VerifyProof;
- the IVM host verify syscalls;
- Torii attachments;
- preverify.

3. Treat any new relation as a loosening. Adding a relation to the list changes the compiled digest. It needs the relation's own W6.R review (or a W6.R.GEN delta review), a qualification and a W6.G record signed by the qualification-signer threshold. CanManageVerifyingKeys alone never adds one.

4. Close the ZV path. The W7.ZV ballot and tally relation goes on the list only through the W7.ZV.Q digest and the W7.ZV.A record. W7.ZV.c keeps the vote-circuit refusal on the non-voting routes on production until then.

5. Add negative tests in W1.6, W1.6i and the W6.H Unavailable leg. On a production-profile network, each of these is rejected with a typed error:
- registering an unlisted FASTPQ AIR;
- a VerifyProof, host-syscall, Torii or preverify call that targets an unlisted relation;
- a ballot-relation proof submitted through VerifyProof before W7.ZV.A.
